{"d":"in","o":{"prompt":"TASK: cards\nJudge the draft as a finished message to Claude. Suggest at most 3 cards, only where one clearly helps; most drafts need none.\nReturn {\"cards\": [...]} — an empty list when the draft is fine. Each card is one of:\n- {\"category\": \"missing\", \"title\": \"<6 words at most>\", \"why\": \"<one line>\", \"edit\": <edit>} — something Claude will need that the draft lacks (which board, which issue, which file, what \"working\" means). The edit adds it when the context names it, else a short question for the engineer in brackets, like [which board?].\n- {\"category\": \"improve\", \"title\": \"<6 words at most>\", \"why\": \"<one line>\", \"edit\": <edit>} — the prompt put better: clearer, more specific, the expected result stated.\n- {\"category\": \"context\", \"kind\": \"compact\" | \"handoff\" | \"background\", \"title\": \"<6 words at most>\", \"why\": \"<one line>\", \"focus\": \"<what the draft is about, 3 to 8 words>\"} — compact when the context is filling (above about 60%) and the draft moves to new work; handoff when the context is nearly full (above about 80%) or the draft starts unrelated work; background when the draft asks for a long, self-contained job (a long build or test run, a sweep, research) that Claude can do while the chat stays free.\nAn edit is {\"op\": \"append\", \"text\": \"...\"}, {\"op\": \"prepend\", \"text\": \"...\"}, or {\"op\": \"replace\", \"find\": \"<an exact substring of the draft>\", \"with\": \"...\"}.\n<desk_references> lists the desk's issues and PRs that may be what the draft means. Check it first: when the draft points at an issue, a PR, a test or a file only vaguely (\"the parser PR\", \"like we did for the parser\", \"that ticket\") and a line there fits, the first card is a missing card titled with that reference, and its edit is {\"op\": \"replace\", \"find\": \"<the vague phrase, exactly as the draft has it>\", \"with\": \"<that phrase naming the reference: its key, the PR as owner/repo#N, and its title>\"} — the reference itself, never a question about it. When several lines fit, one such card per line, at most 3. Only when none fits, ask in brackets. For example, with the line ABC-12 \"Make the parser\" Done · PR owner/repo#7 \"Parser\" merged, the draft \"test it like the parser PR did\" gets {\"category\": \"missing\", \"title\": \"Name PR owner/repo#7\", \"why\": \"The desk knows the parser PR\", \"edit\": {\"op\": \"replace\", \"find\": \"the parser PR\", \"with\": \"PR owner/repo#7 \\\"Parser\\\" (ABC-12)\"}}.\nKeep the engineer's words and voice; change only what the card is about. Never invent facts that are not in the context.\n\n<work_item>ABC-170 Add the gadget parser · In Progress · PR owner/repo#44 \"Gadget parser\" draft</work_item>\n<context_usage>34% of 200K tokens used</context_usage>\n<library_commands>/embarch-chat:embarch-permissions, /embarch-chat:embarch-claude-md, /embarch-chat:build-flash-verify</library_commands>\n<desk_references>\nIssues and PRs on this desk that the draft names, or whose titles share its words, best first:\nABC-154 \"Create the Widget\" Done · PR owner/repo#36 \"Widget\" merged\n</desk_references>\n<claude_last_reply>\nThe gadget parser reads every field now, and its unit test passes. I have not tested its error paths yet.\n</claude_last_reply>\n<draft>Let's include a test just like the widget PR did, with the same structure</draft>"}}
